text For more details on how to use HTML, authors are encouraged to consult tutorials and guides. Some of the examples included in this specification might also be of use, but the novice author is cautioned that this specification, by necessity, defines the language with a level of detail that might be difficult to understand at first.
h3 1.10.1 Writing secure applications with HTML
text This section is non-normative.
text When HTML is used to create interactive sites, care needs to be taken to avoid introducing vulnerabilities through which attackers can compromise the integrity of the site itself or of the site's users.
text A comprehensive study of this matter is beyond the scope of this document, and authors are strongly encouraged to study the matter in more detail. However, this section attempts to provide a quick introduction to some common pitfalls in HTML application development.
text "The security model of the web is based on the concept of \"origins\", and correspondingly many of the potential attacks on the web involve cross-origin actions."
link [ORIGIN] references.html#refsORIGIN
text Not validating user input
text Cross-site scripting (XSS)
text SQL injection
text When accepting untrusted input, e.g. user-generated content such as text comments, values in URL parameters, messages from third-party sites, etc, it is imperative that the data be validated before use, and properly escaped when displayed. Failing to do this can allow a hostile user to perform a variety of attacks, ranging from the potentially benign, such as providing bogus user information like a negative age, to the serious, such as running scripts every time a user looks at a page that includes the information, potentially propagating the attack in the process, to the catastrophic, such as deleting all data in the server.
text When writing filters to validate user input, it is imperative that filters always be safelist-based, allowing known-safe constructs and disallowing all other input. Blocklist-based filters that disallow known-bad inputs and allow everything else are not secure, as not everything that is bad is yet known (for example, because it might be invented in the future).
text For example, suppose a page looked at its URL's query string to determine what to display, and the site then redirected the user to that page to display a message, as in:
text Say Hello  Say Welcome  Say Kittens
card
  text <
  text ul success
  text >
  text <
  text li success
  text ><
  text a success
  text href primary
  text =
  text "\"message.cgi?say=Hello\"" warning
  text >
  text </
  text a success
  text >
  text <
  text li success
  text ><
  text a success
  text href primary
  text =
  text "\"message.cgi?say=Welcome\"" warning
  text >
  text </
  text a success
  text >
  text <
  text li success
  text ><
  text a success
  text href primary
  text =
  text "\"message.cgi?say=Kittens\"" warning
  text >
  text </
  text a success
  text >
  text </
  text ul success
  text >
text If the message was just displayed to the user without escaping, a hostile attacker could then craft a URL that contained a script element:
text "https://example.com/message.cgi?say=%3Cscript%3Ealert%28%27Oh%20no%21%27%29%3C/script%3E"
text If the attacker then convinced a victim user to visit this page, a script of the attacker's choosing would run on the page. Such a script could do any number of hostile actions, limited only by what the site offers: if the site is an e-commerce shop, for instance, such a script could cause the user to unknowingly make arbitrarily many unwanted purchases.
text This is called a cross-site scripting attack.
text There are many constructs that can be used to try to trick a site into executing code. Here are some that authors are encouraged to consider when writing safelist filters:
text When allowing harmless-seeming elements like , it is important to safelist any provided attributes as well. If one allowed all attributes then an attacker could, for instance, use the  attribute to run arbitrary script.
link img embedded-content.html#the-img-element
link onload webappapis.html#handler-onload
text "When allowing URLs to be provided (e.g. for links), the scheme of each URL also needs to be explicitly safelisted, as there are many schemes that can be abused. The most prominent example is \"\", but user agents can implement (and indeed, have historically implemented) others."
link "javascript:" browsing-the-web.html#the-javascript:-url-special-case
text Allowing a  element to be inserted means any  elements in the page with relative links can be hijacked, and similarly that any form submissions can get redirected to a hostile site.
row
  link base semantics.html#the-base-element
  link script scripting.html#the-script-element
text Cross-site request forgery (CSRF)
text If a site allows a user to make form submissions with user-specific side-effects, for example posting messages on a forum under the user's name, making purchases, or applying for a passport, it is important to verify that the request was made by the user intentionally, rather than by another site tricking the user into making the request unknowingly.
text This problem exists because HTML forms can be submitted to other origins.
text Sites can prevent such attacks by populating forms with user-specific hidden tokens, or by checking `` headers on all requests.
link Origin https://fetch.spec.whatwg.org/#http-origin
text Clickjacking
text A page that provides users with an interface to perform actions that the user might not wish to perform needs to be designed so as to avoid the possibility that users can be tricked into activating the interface.
text One way that a user could be so tricked is if a hostile site places the victim site in a small  and then convinces the user to click, for instance by having the user play a reaction game. Once the user is playing the game, the hostile site can quickly position the iframe under the mouse cursor just as the user is about to click, thus tricking the user into clicking the victim site's interface.
link iframe iframe-embed-object.html#the-iframe-element
text To avoid this, sites that do not expect to be used in frames are encouraged to only enable their interface if they detect that they are not in a frame (e.g. by comparing the  object to the value of the  attribute).
row
  link window nav-history-apis.html#dom-window
  link top nav-history-apis.html#dom-top
h3 1.10.2 Common pitfalls to avoid when using the scripting APIs
text This section is non-normative.
text "Scripts in HTML have \"run-to-completion\" semantics, meaning that the browser will generally run the script uninterrupted before doing anything else, such as firing further events or continuing to parse the document."
text On the other hand, parsing of HTML files happens incrementally, meaning that the parser can pause at any point to let scripts run. This is generally a good thing, but it does mean that authors need to be careful to avoid hooking event handlers after the events could have possibly fired.
text There are two techniques for doing this reliably: use , or create the element and add the event handlers in the same script. The latter is safe because, as mentioned earlier, scripts are run to completion before further events can fire.
link "event handler content attributes" webappapis.html#event-handler-content-attributes
text One way this could manifest itself is with  elements and the  event. The event could fire as soon as the element has been parsed, especially if the image has already been cached (which is common).
row
  link img embedded-content.html#the-img-element
  link load indices.html#event-load
text Here, the author uses the  handler on an  element to catch the  event:
row
  link onload webappapis.html#handler-onload
  link img embedded-content.html#the-img-element
  link load indices.html#event-load
card
  text <
  text img success
  text src primary
  text =
  text "\"games.png\"" warning
  text alt primary
  text =
  text "\"Games\"" warning
  text onload primary
  text =
  text "\"gamesLogoHasLoaded(event)\"" warning
  text >
text If the element is being added by script, then so long as the event handlers are added in the same script, the event will still not be missed:
text img   Image imgsrc   imgalt   imgonload  gamesLogoHasLoaded
card
