row
  link "#text" https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#interface-text
  text Then lunch.
link p grouping-content.html#the-p-element
link i text-level-semantics.html#the-i-element
link i text-level-semantics.html#the-i-element
link i text-level-semantics.html#the-i-element
link i text-level-semantics.html#the-i-element
text :
row
  link "#text" https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#interface-text
  text And finally dinner.
text Errors involving fragile syntax constructs
text There are syntax constructs that, for historical reasons, are relatively fragile. To help reduce the number of users who accidentally run into such problems, they are made non-conforming.
text For example, the parsing of certain named character references in attributes happens even with the closing semicolon being omitted. It is safe to include an ampersand followed by letters that do not form a named character reference, but if the letters are changed to a string that does form a named character reference, they will be interpreted as that character instead.
text "In this fragment, the attribute's value is \"?bill&ted\":"
text Bill and Ted
card
  text <
  text a
  text href
  text =
  text "\"?bill&ted\""
  text >
  text </
  text a
  text >
text "In the following fragment, however, the attribute's value is actually \"?art©\", not the intended \"?art&copy\", because even without the final semicolon, \"&copy\" is handled the same as \"&copy;\" and thus gets interpreted as \"©\":"
text Art and Copy
card
  text <
  text a
  text href
  text =
  text "\"?art&copy\""
  text >
  text </
  text a
  text >
text To avoid this problem, all named character references are required to end with a semicolon, and uses of named character references without a semicolon are flagged as errors.
text Thus, the correct way to express the above cases is as follows:
text Bill and Ted
card
  text <
  text a success
  text href primary
  text =
  text "\"?bill&ted\"" warning
  text >
  text </
  text a success
  text >
  text <!-- &ted is ok, since it's not a named character reference -->
text Art and Copy
card
  text <
  text a success
  text href primary
  text =
  text "\"?art&amp;copy\"" warning
  text >
  text </
  text a success
  text >
  text <!-- the & has to be escaped, since &copy
  text is
  text a named character reference -->
text Errors involving known interoperability problems in legacy user agents
text Certain syntax constructs are known to cause especially subtle or serious problems in legacy user agents, and are therefore marked as non-conforming to help authors avoid them.
text For example, this is why the U+0060 GRAVE ACCENT character (`) is not allowed in unquoted attributes. In certain legacy user agents, it is sometimes treated as a quote character.
text Another example of this is the DOCTYPE, which is required to trigger , because the behavior of legacy user agents in  is often largely undocumented.
link "no-quirks mode" https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-document-no-quirks
link "quirks mode" https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-document-quirks
text Errors that risk exposing authors to security attacks
text Certain restrictions exist purely to avoid known security problems.
text For example, the restriction on using UTF-7 exists purely to avoid authors falling prey to a known cross-site-scripting attack using UTF-7.
link [UTF7] references.html#refsUTF7
text Cases where the author's intent is unclear
text Markup where the author's intent is very unclear is often made non-conforming. Correcting these errors early makes later maintenance easier.
text For example, it is unclear whether the author intended the following to be an  heading or an  heading:
row
  link h1 sections.html#the-h1,-h2,-h3,-h4,-h5,-and-h6-elements
  link h2 sections.html#the-h1,-h2,-h3,-h4,-h5,-and-h6-elements
text Contact details
card
  text <
  text h1
  text >
  text </
  text h2
  text >
text Cases that are likely to be typos
text When a user makes a simple typo, it is helpful if the error can be caught early, as this can save the author a lot of debugging time. This specification therefore usually considers it an error to use element names, attribute names, and so forth, that do not match the names defined in this specification.
text For example, if the author typed <capton> instead of <caption>, this would be flagged as an error and the author could correct the typo immediately.
text Errors that could interfere with new syntax in the future
text In order to allow the language syntax to be extended in the future, certain otherwise harmless features are disallowed.
text "For example, \"attributes\" in end tags are ignored currently, but they are invalid, in case a future change to the language makes use of that syntax feature without conflicting with already-deployed (and valid!) content."
text Some authors find it helpful to be in the practice of always quoting all attributes and always including all optional tags, preferring the consistency derived from such custom over the minor benefits of terseness afforded by making use of the flexibility of the HTML syntax. To aid such authors, conformance checkers can provide modes of operation wherein such conventions are enforced.
h3 1.11.3 Restrictions on content models and on attribute values
text This section is non-normative.
text Beyond the syntax of the language, this specification also places restrictions on how elements and attributes can be specified. These restrictions are present for similar reasons:
text Errors involving content with dubious semantics
text To avoid misuse of elements with defined meanings, content models are defined that restrict how elements can be nested when such nestings would be of dubious value.
text For example, this specification disallows nesting a  element inside a  element, since it is highly unlikely for an author to indicate that an entire section should be keyed in.
row
  link section sections.html#the-section-element
  link kbd text-level-semantics.html#the-kbd-element
text Errors that involve a conflict in expressed semantics
text Similarly, to draw the author's attention to mistakes in the use of elements, clear contradictions in the semantics expressed are also considered conformance errors.
text In the fragments below, for example, the semantics are nonsensical: a separator cannot simultaneously be a cell, nor can a radio button be a progress bar.
card
  text <
  text hr
  text role
  text =
  text "\"cell\""
  text >
card
  text <
  text input
  text type
  text =
  text radio
  text role
  text =
  text progressbar
  text >
text Another example is the restrictions on the content models of the  element, which only allows  element children. Lists by definition consist just of zero or more list items, so if a  element contains something other than an  element, it's not clear what was meant.
link ul grouping-content.html#the-ul-element
link li grouping-content.html#the-li-element
link ul grouping-content.html#the-ul-element
link li grouping-content.html#the-li-element
text Cases where the default styles are likely to lead to confusion
text Certain elements have default styles or behaviors that make certain combinations likely to lead to confusion. Where these have equivalent alternatives without this problem, the confusing combinations are disallowed.
text For example,  elements are rendered as , and  elements as . Putting a  in an  is unnecessarily confusing; since either nesting just  elements, or nesting just  elements, or nesting  elements inside  elements all serve the same purpose as nesting a  element in a  element, but only the latter involves a  in an , the latter combination is disallowed.
link div grouping-content.html#the-div-element
link "block boxes" https://drafts.csswg.org/css2/#block-boxes%E2%91%A0
link span text-level-semantics.html#the-span-element
